\section{Introduction}

G\"odel's First Incompleteness Theorem guarantees that any sound, recursively axiomatized theory $T$ extending a modest fragment of arithmetic possesses sentences that are \textbf{independent} of $T$: neither the sentence nor its negation is provable in $T$. The archetype is the consistency statement $\Con(T)$, whose unprovability is the Second Incompleteness Theorem, but there are continuum-many others.

The standard narrative treats such sentences as an epistemic dead end. \emph{G\"odel's Casino} poses this as a decision problem: a dealer presents independent sentences one at a time, and a player must wager TRUE, FALSE, or decline. We ask whether a strategy exists with a positive edge.

Our central observation is that \textbf{independence is not the same as truth-neutrality.} Although $T$ cannot settle an independent sentence \emph{internally}, an external observer who knows the sentence's position in the arithmetic hierarchy can determine its truth value in the standard model $\N$ with certainty. The mechanism is $\Sigma_1$-completeness: true existential statements cannot escape provability. A recurring theme is a \textbf{correction} to folklore: because $\Con(T)$ is a $\Pi_1$ sentence, the correct bet on it is TRUE, not FALSE.

\section{The abstract model of a theory}

\begin{definition}[Theory]
A \emph{theory} $T$ consists of a type $\mathrm{Sentence}$ of sentences; a negation $\neg : \mathrm{Sentence} \to \mathrm{Sentence}$; a provability predicate $\Prov(s)$ (``$T \vdash s$''); a truth predicate $\Tru(s)$ (``$s$ holds in $\N$''); and a classification predicate $\mathrm{IsSigma1}(s)$ (``$s$ is syntactically $\Sigma_1$''); subject to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(Soundness)} $\Prov(s) \Rightarrow \Tru(s)$ for all $s$;
  \item \textbf{(Truth respects negation)} $\Tru(\neg s) \iff \neg\,\Tru(s)$ for all $s$;
  \item \textbf{($\Sigma_1$-completeness)} $\mathrm{IsSigma1}(s) \wedge \Tru(s) \Rightarrow \Prov(s)$ for all $s$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

These are theorems, not assumptions, for any sound recursively axiomatized extension of Robinson arithmetic $Q$. We take them as the defining interface so that the casino analysis is a purely deductive consequence.

\begin{definition}[$\Pi_1$]
A sentence $s$ is \emph{$\Pi_1$}, written $\mathrm{IsPi1}(s)$, iff its negation is $\Sigma_1$: $\mathrm{IsPi1}(s) :\iff \mathrm{IsSigma1}(\neg s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Independence]
A sentence $s$ is \emph{independent} of $T$ iff
$\mathrm{Indep}(s) :\iff \neg\,\Prov(s) \wedge \neg\,\Prov(\neg s).$
\end{definition}

\section{The mathematical core: shape determines truth}

\begin{theorem}[Independent $\Pi_1$ sentences are true]\label{thm:pi1}
Let $s$ be a $\Pi_1$ sentence independent of $T$. Then $\Tru(s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\neg\,\Tru(s)$. By truth-respects-negation, $\Tru(\neg s)$. Since $s$ is $\Pi_1$, $\neg s$ is $\Sigma_1$, so $\Sigma_1$-completeness gives $\Prov(\neg s)$, contradicting independence. Hence $\Tru(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Independent $\Sigma_1$ sentences are false]\label{thm:sigma1}
Let $s$ be a $\Sigma_1$ sentence independent of $T$. Then $\neg\,\Tru(s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\Tru(s)$. Since $s$ is $\Sigma_1$, $\Sigma_1$-completeness gives $\Prov(s)$, contradicting independence. Hence $\neg\,\Tru(s)$.
\end{proof}

Neither proof settles $s$ \emph{inside} $T$---that is impossible by independence. They settle $s$ in the standard model by reasoning \emph{about} $T$. Undecidability is compatible with a definite external truth value, and shape reveals it.

\begin{corollary}[Consistency correction]
If $\Con(T)$, the $\Pi_1$ consistency statement of $T$, is independent of $T$, then it is true; the winning bet is TRUE, not FALSE.
\end{corollary}

\section{The casino and the strategy}

\begin{definition}[Bets and payoffs]
A \emph{bet} is an element of $\{\betTrue, \betFalse, \hedge\}$. The payoff on sentence $s$ is $+1$ for a correct bet, $-1$ for a wrong bet, and $0$ for a hedge:
\[
\mathrm{payoff}(b,s) =
\begin{cases}
+1 & b=\betTrue,\ \Tru(s), \\
-1 & b=\betTrue,\ \neg\Tru(s), \\
-1 & b=\betFalse,\ \Tru(s), \\
+1 & b=\betFalse,\ \neg\Tru(s), \\
0  & b=\hedge.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Kinds and the strategy]
Each card carries a declared kind in $\{\Sigma_1, \Pi_1, \text{other}\}$. The strategy is
$\mathrm{strat}(\Sigma_1)=\betFalse$, $\mathrm{strat}(\Pi_1)=\betTrue$, $\mathrm{strat}(\text{other})=\hedge$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Card]
A \emph{card} is a tuple $(s,k,c,i)$ where $s$ is a sentence, $k$ a declared kind, $c$ a proof $s$ genuinely has kind $k$, and $i$ a proof $s$ is independent of $T$. A card has \emph{decidable shape} iff $k \in \{\Sigma_1,\Pi_1\}$. The card profit is $\mathrm{cardProfit} := \mathrm{payoff}(\mathrm{strat}(k), s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Decidable-shape cards win]\label{thm:cardwin}
A $\Sigma_1$ card returns $+1$; a $\Pi_1$ card returns $+1$; an other card returns $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On $\Sigma_1$ the strategy bets FALSE and the sentence is false (Theorem~\ref{thm:sigma1}): $+1$. On $\Pi_1$ it bets TRUE and the sentence is true (Theorem~\ref{thm:pi1}): $+1$. On other it hedges: $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For every card, $\mathrm{cardProfit} = 1$ if the card has decidable shape and $0$ otherwise; in particular $\mathrm{cardProfit} \ge 0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Deck profit]
For a deck $D=[c_1,\dots,c_N]$, $\ \mathrm{deckProfit}(D)=\sum_{j=1}^N \mathrm{cardProfit}(c_j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Deck profit equals decidable-shape count]\label{thm:count}
$\mathrm{deckProfit}(D) = \#\{ j : c_j \text{ has decidable shape}\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Sum the previous corollary over the deck.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\mathrm{deckProfit}(D) \ge 0$ for every deck, and $\mathrm{deckProfit}(D) \ge 1$ whenever $D$ contains a decidable-shape card.
\end{corollary}

\section{The one-third edge}

\begin{theorem}[One-third guaranteed edge]
Let $D$ be a deck of $N \ge 1$ cards, of which at least $N/3$ have decidable shape. Then $\mathrm{deckProfit}(D)/N \ge 1/3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, $\mathrm{deckProfit}(D)$ equals the number of decidable-shape cards, at least $N/3$; divide by $N$.
\end{proof}

Unlike ``positive expected value,'' this is a floor with no downside risk: every round is $0$ or $+1$, and at least a third are $+1$.

\section{Duality: the naive strategy loses}

Let $\mathrm{strat}^{\text{naive}}$ bet FALSE on $\Pi_1$ and TRUE on $\Sigma_1$, hedging otherwise.

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise inversion]
For every card, $\mathrm{naiveCardProfit} = -\,\mathrm{cardProfit}$; hence $\mathrm{naiveDeckProfit}(D) = -\,\mathrm{deckProfit}(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On $\Sigma_1$ the naive bet TRUE loses since the sentence is false: $-1$. On $\Pi_1$ the naive bet FALSE loses since the sentence is true: $-1$. On other it hedges: $0$. Each is the negation of $\mathrm{cardProfit}$.
\end{proof}

The house edge is real and directional: knowing the correct direction is precisely what separates winning from losing.

\section{A concrete non-vacuous instance}

\begin{construction}[Toy theory]
Take $\mathrm{Sentence}=\{\mathsf{t},\mathsf{f}\}$ with $\neg\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{f}$, $\neg\mathsf{f}=\mathsf{t}$; let $\Tru(\mathsf{t})$ hold and $\Tru(\mathsf{f})$ fail; let $\Prov$ be identically false; and let $\mathrm{IsSigma1}$ hold of $\mathsf{f}$ only.
\end{construction}

\begin{proposition}
The toy theory satisfies all axioms of the Theory definition. Soundness holds vacuously; truth-respects-negation by case check; $\Sigma_1$-completeness vacuously, as the only $\Sigma_1$ atom $\mathsf{f}$ is not true. Then $\mathsf{t}$ is an independent $\Pi_1$ card, and the single-card deck has $\mathrm{deckProfit}=1$. Hence the casino theorems are non-vacuous.
\end{proposition}

\section{Discussion and future work}

The philosophical upshot is that \textbf{unprovability and unknowability come apart.} G\"odel's theorems constrain what a theory can establish about itself, but do not forbid an external analyst from determining truth values of independent sentences of simple form. The result is deterministic (no round loses), constructive (the bet is read off the kind), and robust (its three hypotheses hold for every sound recursively axiomatized arithmetic theory).

Future directions include: (1) grounding the abstract theory in genuine first-order arithmetic and proving $\Sigma_1$-completeness of $Q$/PA as a theorem; (2) building an explicit deck of famous cards ($\Con(\mathrm{ZFC})$, a Rosser $\Sigma_1$ sentence, the Continuum Hypothesis as a hedged ``other''); (3) proving minimax-optimality of the hedge on ``other'' cards; and (4) adding a genuine probabilistic layer recovering the original expected-value framing as a corollary.

\section{Conclusion}

G\"odel's Casino reframes incompleteness from a boundary into a betting table. Independent $\Pi_1$ sentences are true and independent $\Sigma_1$ sentences are false; the strategy---bet TRUE on $\Pi_1$, FALSE on $\Sigma_1$, hedge otherwise---never loses, and total profit equals the count of single-quantifier cards. The impossible game is winnable, provided one plays it from outside the system.

